A||N||6||BJW||SHIFTIN' the damn GETRAG 5 speed into 1st!!!||||13||
Z||000000||BJW||10-21-2000||01:33 AM||||yo . . . guys and gals with Getrag 5-speeds:<P>I have an '87 GT with the 5-speed . . . high miles but still runs strong . . . <P>two open questions:<P>1) what causes the tranny NOT to be able to be shifted into 1st. sometimes? I have new fluid in the tranny, and the cables are tight and lubed OK . . . also the shifter in the console is fairly tight too . . . I try shifting it into 2nd or 3rd first - sometimes it helps . . . also double clutching . . . but why does it do this sporadically??? - most of the time, 1st is NO PROBLEM . . . <P>2) problem numero duo, was posted a couple of weeks ago . . . <BR>high idle (1300 rpm) triggers a code 35 (IAC) and the check engine light comes on at stoplights after the 30 seconds at the too high idle (I know that the computer waits 30 seconds before setting the trouble code - if the idle is + or - 50 rpm . . . I have NO vacuum leaks, the IAC is NEW (and seems to be seating OK), the throttle butterfly is closing all the way and is clean, and the TPS is also fairly new . . . arrrrggg!!!!<BR>could it be the MAP sensor? I have a theory that one of the temperature sensors must be defective - but which one would affect the idle this way? . . . there is an outside chance that the IAC 'pintle' is not seating all the way - does anyone know if disconnecting the IAC wiring harness would allow the IAC valve to remain completely shut? . . . I know it would still trigger a code 35, but if the idle DROPPED to the proper level, the problem would be isolated to the IAC . . . <BR>how DAMNED frustrating . . . Oh, and when the idle is at 1300 when the engine is warm, the NEW catalytic convertor REALLY stinks . . . could this mean an overly lean or rich mixture?<BR>B. in Toronto||||reg||13
Z||000001||mrfiero||10-21-2000||01:55 AM||||The shifting problem could be anything.....maybe a binding TO bearing or bad hydraulics.  It sounds like you have everything in good shape, so it really is hard to say (especially not being able to see it in person).<P>As for the high idle and code 35....it is your EGR tube/pipe.  The easiest way to test it is to start it up and let it idle.....take a can of WD-40 (or equivalent) and spray the ends of the pipe where it connects to the EGR valve spacer and the plenum.  If the idle changes at all (should rise) then you have a crack.  They are a little bit of a pain to change, but it isn't that hard of a job....just a little pricey.  New ones are about $100, but you may find a good used one to put in. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||FIEROCIOUS||10-21-2000||04:29 PM||||While down shifting? How fast are you trying to force the shift lever into first? Sometimes the sincros need a little more time to synconize. Try shifting a (little) slower.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ed||10-21-2000||04:45 PM||||This topic (shifting) has been seen here before.  I think the consensus was that the 5-speeds don't like going into first (design flaw).  Maybe we should sue and get a judge to put out a recall.  And we can get our ignition modules recalled at the same time.  But I digress. <P>Mine doesn't like to shift into first either, enought that my wife complains about it.  I adjusted it at the shift lever which helped a little.  <P>I just shift into first while rolling, or go through second, or start rolling in second and then go into first.<P>Be glad you don't have an old English sports car with non-syncro first.  Hard shifting and transmission damage were so common that a professional mechanic thought it was OK to have grinding in first gear (which cost me a bunch of money later when I HAD to replace the gears). ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Patrick's Dad||10-22-2000||03:10 PM||||Hmmm... Maybe I shouldn't say anything so you guys don't come and raid my garage....<P>This is the first I've heard of the Getrag not shifting into 1st. I've never had this problem (131K), and I suppose that I don't want to do anything that will change the setup when I pull it and bolt it to a different motor....<P>I did have this problem with my first 5speed, which was a 1979 280ZX. Not knowing anything about it, I just used a brute force karate chop to the lever. The car went "clunk," but the gear worked. So did the others. I seem to remember that it was easier to get it into first while rolling, as mentioned above.<P>I'll have to keep track of this convo, for responses of those more knowledgeable than I.<P>------------------<BR>This should work until my site comes online....   <IMG SRC="http://homestead.deja.com/user.rshuman/files/Tmh.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||bHooper||10-22-2000||08:35 PM||||i have never noticed a problem shifting my getrag into first.  but then again, i am on my 2nd tranny and 4th clutch in 11 years  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>ironically, everyone else that try's to drive the thing has a hard time getting into first... they seem to find third a lot easier (mostly when the car is stopped).  for this reason, my girlfriend has been told she cant drive the car without me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||10
Z||000006||PJ||10-22-2000||11:01 PM||||Are you using the Synchromesh fluid? It has slip additives in it that you won't find in regular 5W30 gear oil.<P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black<BR>||||reg||1
